# Copy to .env and fill in. NOTE: every VITE_* value is inlined into the built
# JavaScript and is therefore PUBLIC to anyone who loads the site. No ClickUp
# credential belongs in this file — n8n holds it server-side.

# n8n webhook (GET) that returns the open positions. It should read ClickUp list
# 901220480011 ("Positions", workspace 9012912728) filtered to status "live" and
# return the tasks with their custom_fields intact — either the ClickUp response
# verbatim ({ "tasks": [...] }) or a bare array of task objects. The workflow that
# serves it lives in n8n/careers-positions.workflow.json.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/careers-positions
VITE_JOBS_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Basic auth for the above. These are public in the bundle, so they deter casual
# scraping only and are not a security boundary — keep the endpoint read-only.
# The n8n Webhook node must have Basic Auth enabled for them to mean anything.
VITE_JOBS_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_JOBS_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# The public origin this site is served from. Used for canonical URLs and for
# the JobPosting structured data on each role. Defaults to https://aidapt.co.
VITE_SITE_URL=

# n8n webhook (POST, multipart/form-data) that receives application submissions.
# It MUST re-check on the server what the browser can only suggest: that the
# openPosition id is a currently live role, that resumeFile and photo are the
# declared types and under 5MB, and that the request is rate limited per IP.
# See n8n/README.md.
VITE_APPLICATION_WEBHOOK_URL=
VITE_APPLICATION_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_APPLICATION_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# New job requisition (internal, /requisitions/new)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# n8n webhook (GET) that serves the form schema: every option list keyed by the
# stable field keys in src/requisition/schema.ts, plus the ClickUp member
# directory. n8n reads GET /api/v2/list/901220480011/field, caches it, and maps
# each ClickUp field onto a key. No ClickUp field id, list id or token reaches
# the browser. A key the workspace cannot supply must be reported in
# "missingKeys" — the form then refuses to open rather than silently dropping
# whatever the manager typed into it.
# For local work without n8n, point this at the checked-in fixture:
#   VITE_REQUISITION_SCHEMA_URL=/requisition-schema.sample.json
VITE_REQUISITION_SCHEMA_URL=

# n8n webhook (POST, application/json) that receives a completed requisition.
# It resolves labels to option UUIDs, creates the task in the Positions list
# with status Draft and tag "requisition", stamps Requisition Raised, and
# returns { taskId, taskUrl }. It must reject a repeated submissionId — a
# manager double-clicking Submit must not raise two requisitions.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/job-requisition-form-submission
VITE_REQUISITION_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional basic auth, shared by both requisition endpoints. Public in the
# bundle, so this deters casual traffic only.
VITE_REQUISITION_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_REQUISITION_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# The requesting-manager picker. Returns the employee register with avatars.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/kenafric/employees-with-avatars
VITE_EMPLOYEES_WEBHOOK_URL=

# "Reports to" and the interview panel picker. Returns the user list.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/kenafric/users
VITE_USERS_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fill the requisition form with sample content in one click, for reviewing
# the form with real text in it. Always on in development; set this only on a
# preview deployment.
#
# Leave it UNSET in production. The sample content is invented, and a
# requisition raised from it would arrive in ClickUp looking like a real one.
VITE_ALLOW_PREFILL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Employee onboarding (/onboarding/{clickupTaskId})
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The new hire has no ClickUp account and no password. The link WF-15 emails
# them carries their ClickUp Employee task id — /onboarding/869evrmhx, or
# ?id=869evrmhx — and that id is what identifies them. The app reads it, sends
# it to the session endpoint as ?id=, and renders whatever comes back.
#
# A ClickUp task id is short and enumerable, so it is a name rather than a
# secret. The safeguards therefore have to live in n8n and are not optional:
# rate-limit hard per id and per IP, serve a session ONLY for an employee at
# status preboarding with documents still outstanding, and log every hit.
# n8n/onboarding-token.cjs holds a signed-link implementation if that is ever
# judged too thin.

# n8n webhook (GET, ?id={clickupTaskId}) that returns THAT ONE employee's
# details: name, joining date, position, company, which conditional documents
# apply to them, which documents are already Received, and the manifest of what
# this submissionId has already uploaded. Never a list.
#
# ?id= is the parameter name the app sends. It MUST answer 404 (or 403/410) for
# anything that is not a live onboarding record, and it MUST rate limit.
# For local work without n8n, point this at the checked-in fixture:
#   VITE_ONBOARDING_SESSION_URL=/onboarding-session.sample.json
# Any id then opens the form, because a static file cannot look anything up —
# so that value belongs on a local or preview build only.
VITE_ONBOARDING_SESSION_URL=

# n8n webhook (POST, multipart/form-data) that takes the WHOLE submission:
# a `payload` field carrying the JSON in docs/onboarding-submission-1.0.schema.json,
# plus one part per document named file0, file1, ... mapped by documents[].field.
#
# It creates the task in Onboarding Submissions at status "new" named
# "{fullName} — Onboarding", writes the personal fields, attaches each
# document, and hands off to WF-15 Trigger B. It must reject a repeated
# submissionId, and it must return only { ok, submissionId, taskId } — the
# employee has no ClickUp account, so a ClickUp URL shown to them reads as a
# broken system.
#
# Raise n8n's N8N_PAYLOAD_SIZE_MAX to match LIMITS.totalBytes in
# src/onboarding/contract.ts (16 MB by default, which is also n8n's default).
# The app blocks the submit above that figure rather than letting the webhook
# refuse it after a four-minute upload.
VITE_ONBOARDING_SUBMIT_URL=

# Optional basic auth shared by the three endpoints above. Public in the
# bundle, so this deters casual traffic only and is not a security boundary.
VITE_ONBOARDING_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_ONBOARDING_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# Kenafric's privacy notice, linked beside the consent checkbox on the
# onboarding form, and the HR address offered when a document is too large to
# upload. Both are UNSET by default and are never guessed: that screen asks for
# a national ID, bank details and a photograph of somebody's face, and a wrong
# privacy link on it is worse than no link. The app renders each only when it
# is configured, so leaving these empty degrades quietly rather than lying.
VITE_ONBOARDING_PRIVACY_URL=
VITE_ONBOARDING_HR_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feedback forms
#   /feedback/candidate-review            F1 / F2  candidate recruitment review
#   /feedback/manager-recruitment-review  F3       manager recruitment review
#   /feedback/new-hire-readiness          F4       new-hire readiness, Month 1 / Month 3
#   /feedback/employee-check-in           F5       employee check-in, Day 30 to Day 180
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The link WF-22 emails carries a SIGNED token: /feedback/candidate-review?t=<token>.
# The app never decodes it and never trusts it — see src/feedback/session.ts.
# n8n verifies the HMAC and the expiry against LINK_SECRET (the same secret
# already used by WF-11d/11e — reuse it, do not mint a second one) and answers
# with that one person's prefilled details.
#
# Which form is rendered, and whether the payroll block appears, is decided by
# the `formType` the CONTEXT endpoint returns. Never by the token's own claims,
# which a candidate could rewrite, and never by the URL.

# n8n webhook (GET, ?t=<token>) that verifies the token and returns
# { ok, formType, alreadySubmitted, prefill: { fullName, email, payroll,
# positionTitle, jobTitle, company, department, recruitmentType, reviewPoint } }.
#
# ?t= is the parameter name the app sends. It MUST answer 401/403 for a bad
# signature, 410 for an expired or spent token and 404 for a token that refers
# to nothing — the app renders all of those as one dead end on purpose, so the
# screen never tells somebody holding a guessed token which part was wrong.
#
# prefill carries fullName (ALWAYS whoever is filling the form in — the
# candidate on F1/F2, the manager on F3/F4), subjectName (the new hire being
# reviewed, F4 only), hiresMade ("2 of 2", F3 only) and reviewPoint
# ("Month 1" / "Month 3", F4 only).
#
# For local work without n8n, point this at one of the checked-in fixtures —
# one per instrument, because each returns a different formType:
#   VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=/feedback-context.sample.json                       F1
#   VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=/feedback-context-internal.sample.json              F2
#   VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=/feedback-context-manager-review.sample.json        F3
#   VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=/feedback-context-new-hire-readiness.sample.json    F4
#   VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=/feedback-context-employee-check-in.sample.json     F5
# Any token then opens the form, because a static file cannot verify one — so
# that value belongs on a local or preview build only. A page whose spec does
# not serve the formType it is handed renders the dead end, not the wrong form.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/kenafric-feedback-context
VITE_FEEDBACK_CONTEXT_URL=

# n8n webhook (POST, application/json) that receives a completed response:
# { t, formType, submittedAt, answers }, where `answers` is keyed by ClickUp
# custom field id. See docs/feedback-submission-1.0.schema.json.
#
# WF-21 resolves every drop_down NAME to its option UUID against the live
# schema, computes Overall Rating, and creates the task in Feedback Responses
# (901220480198) at status "new response".
#
# The token is the idempotency key: WF-21 must refuse a Response Token already
# present on the list and answer 409, which the app treats as a success —
# because it is one. That is also what makes an n8n retry harmless.
# Production: https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/kenafric-wf21
# (renumbered from the old path on 2026-09-10 — WF-14/WF-15 belong to onboarding)
VITE_FEEDBACK_SUBMIT_URL=

# Optional basic auth shared by both endpoints above. Public in the bundle, so
# this deters casual traffic only and is not a security boundary — the token's
# signature is the actual boundary.
VITE_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The n8n host every workflow in this system is served from.
#
# Host and path are separate so that pointing the whole layer at a staging n8n
# is ONE variable rather than one per endpoint. No trailing slash needed —
# config.ts strips one if you leave it on, because a doubled slash makes n8n
# answer with the same "webhook is not registered" 404 as a workflow nobody
# activated, and the two are indistinguishable from the outside.
#
# Every path below is a PRODUCTION path — /webhook/…. n8n also serves a
# /webhook-test/… URL for each workflow, but only while somebody has the editor
# open with "Listen for test event" pressed, and only for one request. Use those
# by hand when stepping through a workflow; NEVER commit one and never set one
# on a deployment, because it works for whoever is listening and 404s for
# everybody else.
#
# LINK_SECRET is deliberately absent from this file and from the app. The app
# carries ?t= through and neither mints nor verifies a token — n8n does that,
# server-side. Every VITE_ value is inlined into the bundle and is public.
#
# Leave unset to use the live host.
VITE_N8N_BASE_URL=https://aidapt.app.n8n.cloud

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Probation KPIs
#   /kpi/define   Build E   the line manager sets 3-5 KPIs, weights total 100
#   /kpi/review   Build F   mid-probation and final review, one route, two modes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The link WF-18a (define) or WF-26a (review) emails carries a SIGNED token:
# /kpi/define?t=<token>. The app never decodes it and never trusts it — see
# src/kpi/session.ts. n8n verifies the HMAC and the expiry against LINK_SECRET
# (the SAME secret as WF-11d/11e and WF-21..25 — reuse it, do not mint a
# second one) and answers with that one employee's details.
#
# Which form is rendered, whether the scoring block appears, and whether the
# panel adjustment appears are all decided by what the CONTEXT endpoint
# returns — never by the token's own claims, and never by the URL. A manager
# who could edit mode=mid to mode=final in the address bar could score
# somebody three months early.
#
# No ClickUp field id, option UUID or list id is configured here or carried in
# either payload: both are keyed by meaning, and the workflow resolves the
# eight ids once. See docs/kpi-definition-1.0.schema.json and
# docs/kpi-review-1.0.schema.json.

# n8n webhook (GET, ?t=<token>) serving BOTH forms. It returns
# { ok, formType, alreadySubmitted, prefill }, where prefill carries
# employeeName, positionTitle, department, company, lineManager, joiningDate,
# probationEndDate, reviewCycle, plus:
#   mode  null on a KPID context; "mid" or "final" on a KPIR one. A KPIR
#         context with no mode is REFUSED by the app rather than guessed at.
#   kpis  [] on a KPID context; the agreed set on a KPIR one, each row
#         carrying taskId, keyResultArea, kpi, measurementType, howMeasured,
#         targetFigure, unitOfMeasure, target, weight, status, and (on a
#         final) the progress and midReviewNotes the mid review left.
#         A row with status "void" is dropped by the app — a voided KPI comes
#         out of the numerator and the denominator both.
#   hr    true only for an HR token; it is what renders the panel adjustment.
#
# ?t= is the parameter name the app sends. It MUST answer 401/403 for a bad
# signature, 410 for an expired or spent token and 404 for a token that refers
# to nothing — the app renders all of those as one dead end on purpose, so the
# screen never tells somebody holding a guessed token which part was wrong,
# and never names the employee.
#
# For local work without n8n there is no fixture URL to point at: the KPI
# forms carry their own sample contexts, reached with ?mock= on either route
# (see src/kpi/mock.ts). ?mock= is honoured in development and on a build with
# VITE_ALLOW_PREFILL=true, and NOTHING submitted from a mocked page leaves the
# browser.
# Path only — the host comes from VITE_N8N_BASE_URL above.
VITE_KPI_CONTEXT_PATH=/webhook/kenafric-kpi-context

# n8n webhook (POST, application/json) — WF-18b, a new KPI set.
# { t, formType: "KPID", submittedAt, kpis[] }. It creates one Probation KPIs
# task per KPI at status "in progress", links the employee both ways, assigns
# the line manager, sets KPI Submission = Submitted and closes the "Email to
# line manager for KPIs" subtask. The token is the idempotency key: it is
# stored as KPI Set Token, and a set already on the list must answer 409 —
# which the app treats as the success it is.
# Path only.
VITE_KPI_DEFINE_PATH=/webhook/kenafric-wf18b

# n8n webhook (POST, application/json) — WF-26b, a mid or final review.
# { t, formType: "KPIR", mode, submittedAt, kpis[], talent? }.
#
# The payload carries NO scores. WF-26b computes Score, Weighted KPI Score and
# Final Individual Score, and Weighted Score is a ClickUp formula that
# recomputes itself — two sources of truth for the number a probation decision
# rests on is one too many. It appends KMID or KFIN to Automation Markers
# (append, never replace: WF-25 uses that field too) and refuses a review
# whose marker is already there.
# Path only.
VITE_KPI_REVIEW_PATH=/webhook/kenafric-wf26b

# Optional basic auth shared by all three endpoints above. Public in the
# bundle, so this deters casual traffic only — the token's signature is the
# actual boundary.
VITE_KPI_WEBHOOK_USER=
VITE_KPI_WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=
